  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="header">
      <a class="header-brand" href="@@SITE_URL@@">
        <span class="header-brand-text">@@SITE_NAME@@</span>
      </a>
    </header>

    <main class="content-container">
      <div class="content">
        <div class="intro">
          <h1>Data dumps</h1>
          <p>
            To meet the requests of archivers, statisticians, and data analysts alike, @@SITE_NAME@@ provides dumps of
            its database on a regular basis. These data consist only of publicly accessible database contents. The
            dumps are provided in <code class="code-span">pg_dump</code>'s custom format, and are created with
            PostgreSQL @@POSTGRES_VERSION@@.
          </p>
        </div>

        <section class="block">
          <header class="block-header">
            <span class="block-header-title">Download</span>
            <div class="block-header-actions"><a href="latest.json">latest.json</a></div>
          </header>
          <div class="block-content">
            <div class="table-wrap responsive">
              <table class="table download">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Link</th>
                    <th>Size</th>
                    <th>Created</th>
                    <th>SHA-256</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td><a href="@@FILE@@">@@FILE@@</a></td>
                    <td title="@@SIZE_BYTES@@ bytes">@@SIZE@@</td>
                    <td><time datetime="@@CREATED_AT@@">@@CREATED@@</time></td>
                    <td class="checksum">@@SHA256@@</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p>
              Note: these dumps do not include images. <a href="latest.json">latest.json</a> has the name, the size
              and the checksum of the newest dump, for scripts.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <h2>Loading a dump</h2>
        <p>To load an entire dump (requires @@DATABASE_SIZE@@ of free storage):</p>
        <pre class="code-block">dropdb --if-exists @@DATABASE@@
createdb @@DATABASE@@
pg_restore -O -d @@DATABASE@@ @@FILE@@</pre>
        <p>
          The <code class="code-span">pg_restore</code> used has to be of PostgreSQL @@POSTGRES_VERSION@@ or newer.
          Finer granularity over the imported data can be controlled with its flags; see
          <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgrestore.html">the documentation</a> for more detail.
        </p>

        <h2>Table layout</h2>
        <p>Loading a dump creates the database <code class="code-span">@@DATABASE@@</code> with these tables:</p>
        <div class="schema">
<!-- TABLES -->
        </div>

        <footer class="site-footer">
          <p>
            Updated <time datetime="@@CREATED_AT@@">@@CREATED@@</time> · styled with
            <a href="https://github.com/Meow/PhilomenaCSS">PhilomenaCSS</a>
          </p>
        </footer>
      </div>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
